 def _label_for(key):
     """How a card's identity is spoken in messages and logs."""
     return "request %s" % key if is_request_key(key) else "PR #%s" % key
+
+
+# The keyword the fix PR carries so that merging it closes the escalation report
+# it answers. GitHub treats Closes/Fixes/Resolves identically; one is named here
+# so every brief says the same thing and a reviewer has one string to look for.
+CLOSING_KEYWORD = "Closes"
+
+
+def escalation_autoclose_instruction(key):
+    """What to tell the builder so merging the fix closes the escalation report.
+
+    Returns the instruction, or None when the card has no escalation PR to close.
+
+    `key` is the decision card's own key, taken AFTER canonicalisation in
+    decide(). A PR-keyed card is keyed by the pull request its finding was
+    reported on, so for those `key` IS the escalation report -- it is the linked
+    escalation by construction, and no path through here can name a different
+    pull request. In particular it is never the fix's own number: the fix does
+    not exist yet when this runs.
+
+    A request card (`req-...`) returns None. It has no escalation PR -- it was
+    raised from a City Hall request before any pull request existed -- so there
+    is nothing to close, and inventing a number would be far worse than saying
+    nothing. That branch keeps its existing behaviour untouched.
+
+    WHY THIS WORKS, AND WHY NOT TO "CORRECT" IT BACK. GitHub's closing keywords
+    close a referenced PULL REQUEST, not only an issue. Verified by execution
+    against this repository on 2026-09-23, twice: a scratch PR whose body carried
+    `Closes #22` closed PR #22 on merge, and a replication carrying `Fixes #24`
+    closed PR #24 -- which had a real diff, so it was not closed for being empty.
+    Both ClosedEvents name the merging pull request as `closer`, stateReason
+    COMPLETED.
+
+    Two GitHub APIs report this as not happening, and both are wrong about the
+    outcome: `closingIssuesReferences` is typed IssueConnection, so a PR cannot
+    appear in it whatever the link, and `CrossReferencedEvent.willCloseTarget`
+    read false on a reference that then closed its target. They are silent, not
+    negative. If this ever looks wrong, re-run the live test rather than
+    reasoning from those two fields.
+
+    Takes effect only on merge into the DEFAULT branch, which is the only way
+    anything lands here: `main` is protected and nothing reaches it except
+    through a pull request (AGENTS-CHARTER.md S2).
+    """
+    if is_request_key(key):
+        return None
+    return (
+        "Put `%s #%d` on its own line in the new pull request's DESCRIPTION -- "
+        "the body, not a commit message and not a comment. #%d is the escalation "
+        "report this fix answers, and that line is what closes it automatically "
+        "when the fix merges. Without it somebody has to close #%d by hand, "
+        "which is the exact thing this line exists to prevent, so treat it as "
+        "part of the change and not as a formality."
+        % (CLOSING_KEYWORD, key, key, key)
+    )
 
 
